  {
   "id": "root",
   "x": 610,
   "y": 10,
   "w": 720,
   "kind": "dark",
   "title": "Adrenal and pituitary disease: which hormone system, then is it too LOW or too HIGH?",
   "lines": [
    "always ask about exogenous steroid"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "g_cort",
   "x": 45,
   "y": 120,
   "w": 780,
   "kind": "plain",
   "title": "CORTISOL AXIS: both glands, ACTH tells the level",
   "lines": [
    "hypothalamus (CRH) → pituitary (ACTH) → adrenal (cortisol)",
    "~a fault at the adrenal is PRIMARY, at the pituitary SECONDARY"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "g_adr",
   "x": 859,
   "y": 120,
   "w": 516,
   "kind": "plain",
   "title": "ADRENAL ONLY: no pituitary control",
   "lines": [
    "aldosterone runs on renin · catecholamines on the sympathetic nerves",
    "~an incidentaloma is screened for cortisol, aldosterone and catecholamines"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "g_pit",
   "x": 1409,
   "y": 120,
   "w": 516,
   "kind": "plain",
   "title": "PITUITARY ONLY: its own hormones and its mass",
   "lines": [
    "one screening test per hormone",
    "~always formal visual fields · rare TSH adenoma below"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "t_lo",
   "x": 45,
   "y": 244,
   "w": 516,
   "kind": "plain",
   "title": "Too LITTLE cortisol?",
   "lines": [
    "AM cortisol 6–9 am + paired ACTH",
    "~a random cortisol is uninterpretable"
   ],
   "h": 96
  },
  {
   "id": "t_hi",
   "x": 573,
   "y": 244,
   "w": 252,
   "kind": "plain",
   "title": "Too MUCH cortisol?",
   "lines": [
    "two positive screens, then ACTH:",
    "normal/↑ → PITUITARY (or ectopic)",
    "suppressed → ADRENAL"
   ],
   "h": 96
  },
  {
